WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:18.029
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls. Ladies
and germs. This is, I forgot there was a camera.

00:00:18.029 --> 00:00:23.689
This is Tim Ferriss. Welcome to another episode
of the Tim Ferriss Show. We have, yet again,

00:00:23.689 --> 00:00:26.789
"The Random Show." Yes. Kev, Kev Rosero. Kevin
Rose.

00:00:26.789 --> 00:00:29.029
Kevin Rose: Thank you for having me, Tim.
It's good to be here.

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:30.820
Tim Ferriss: It's good to be here in your
home.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:35.090
Kevin Rose: We're in a home.
Tim Ferriss: We have our matching mini scooters.

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:38.070
Kevin Rose: We do.
Tim Ferriss: We also have a miniature kitchen

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:42.630
set behind us. We were making pinto beans
and homestyle ice cream. You may or may not

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.320
be able to see that.
Kevin Rose: Their favorite topping for pinto

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.030
beans and —
Tim Ferriss: And by them, he means his progeny.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:51.879
Kevin Rose: I have a couple little ones that
hopefully will not be making an appearance

00:00:51.879 --> 00:00:53.800
and have gone to bed, but we shall see. We
already had a break —

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.059
Tim Ferriss: We already had a princess on
the run. A little jail break. Who came flying

00:00:57.059 --> 00:00:58.980
through? So Kevin, before recording, actually
while recording, you said this is the important

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:00.140
juice.
Kevin Rose: Yes.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:08.080
Tim Ferriss: But that was off mic, so I relayed
it into the mic. What is this important juice

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.540
that we have before us?
Kevin Rose: So, I mean, we've been doing these

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.450
shows for a very long time.
Tim Ferriss: Super long.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:16.150
Kevin Rose: We've always, well, not always.
We've highlighted some of our favorite drinks

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:22.329
over the years and one of the things that
I — comes into my possession about once

00:01:22.329 --> 00:01:26.750
a year is a limited edition run.
Tim Ferriss: You know it's expensive when

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:30.790
it's in a passive sentence form. Comes into
my possession.

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:35.240
Kevin Rose: That's right. It sounds very fancy.
It's just a beer, people. So one of the things

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:40.369
that I like to do, and we've talked about
this before at some point, but unlike wine,

00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:45.330
the cool thing about beer is you can buy the
best beer in the world, the number one ranked

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:46.760
beer, and it's going to cost you a hundred
dollars.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.890
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Kevin Rose: That's not the case with wine,

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:49.979
obviously.
Tim Ferriss: No.

00:01:49.979 --> 00:01:56.700
Kevin Rose: So, in terms of different styles
of beer, obviously it's typically not IPAs

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:02.700
that win the number one slot on that, it's
some of the heavier stuff. But on the IPA

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:09.009
front, the second highest ranked IPA by Beer
Advocate, and I would argue the harder to

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:15.660
find version of the IPAs. Because the number-one
ranked IPA is called Heady Topper, and it

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:19.690
is in production year round.
This particular beer by Russian River Brewing

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:25.310
is called Pliny the Younger. Now you may have
heard Pliny the Elder, which is, you typically

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:29.200
find at Whole Foods and things like that.
The Younger is a limited edition run that

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.629
they do once a year and just, you used to
have to go to Russian River Brewing to actually

00:02:34.629 --> 00:02:39.540
consume it. And just recently, probably, let's
say three or four years ago, they started

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:46.340
putting it in bottles. So this is it in bottle
form. I would argue that this is probably

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:52.450
the most sought-after IPA in the world. It
is $75 a bottle, so it's not cheap. It is

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:58.690
a 16-ounce bottle, though, so per ounce. Nope,
it's a, it's one pint. Yeah, one pint. So

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:04.690
it is, it's expensive juice, but it is quite
good. And I wanted to share it with you.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:07.120
Tim Ferriss: Thank you.
Kevin Rose: And I also wanted to tell people

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.980
out there how to find the rarest beers in
the world.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.430
Tim Ferriss: Let's do it.
Kevin Rose: I may have mentioned this at one

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:17.510
crazy, long-ago Random Show, but there is
a place that I go to called MyBeerCollectibles.com.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:22.990
This isn't an ad. I have nothing to do with
them. Every time I just mention any URL, you're

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:24.900
in the same boat!
Kevin Rose: Checkout code Kev2023.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:29.930
Tim Ferriss: Exactly. Exactly. Make sure you
get my 20 percent. But one of the things that,

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:34.909
this is what I'm probably going to get them
arrested, but one of the things that is very

00:03:34.909 --> 00:03:41.150
difficult to do is to sell alcohol online.
And so on the beer front, if you say, and

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:45.750
this is true, if you were to drink this bottle,
you can sell the bottle by itself for let's

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:49.819
just say $5 or whatever it may be, because
the bottle is just collectible in its own

00:03:49.819 --> 00:03:55.620
right. And so what they claim is that everything
you're buying is a collectible, and so you're

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:59.939
not actually buying the beer.
And so it's a peer-to-peer marketplace. And

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:02.600
so they have people that go and buy that.
I've been using them for several years now.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:05.790
Tim Ferriss: They're going to have the best
month of sales before they get shut down.

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:10.700
It's going to be really a blaze of glory.
Kevin Rose: They've been okay. They've been

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:14.799
okay. And I always find really good sellers
on there. And you see on the side of this,

00:04:14.799 --> 00:04:19.980
it says, "Not packaged for resale." So you
have to know somebody to get this. And yeah,

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:24.670
I love a good IPA. And I would say this is
up there. This is pretty good stuff.

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:30.120
TIm Ferriss: You know the wine world. You
know probably something about the beer world.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:34.830
You also know the high-end — I'm not even
going to call them watches, God forbid — timepiece

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:35.860
world —
Kevin Rose: Sure.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:49.389
Tim Ferriss: — very well. How much of the
fame that comes from being sought after is

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:55.281
derived from the quality of the product versus
the scarcity versus the positioning, if there

00:04:55.281 --> 00:05:01.620
is such a thing? Marketing, other factors.
I've become really interested in this question,

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:05.410
and there's actually a spectacular episode,
I haven't listened to it yet, on LVMH and

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:11.580
the building of that from its very meager
beginnings to its insane current state. And

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:16.090
there's a book called How Luxury Lost Its
Luster, so a lot of alliteration, which I

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:21.889
read a long time ago. I do find the study
of sort of scarcity and high-end products

00:05:21.889 --> 00:05:24.419
very interesting.
Kevin Rose: Have you read the book The Luxury

00:05:24.419 --> 00:05:26.949
Strategy?
Tim Ferriss: The Luxury of Strategy?

00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:28.430
Kevin Rose: No. The Luxury Strategy.
Tim Ferriss: The Luxury Strategy. I was like,

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:32.680
The Luxury of Strategy? It seems like a necessity.
No, I haven't.

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:36.360
Kevin Rose: It's actually another really good
one. It talks about just brand positioning

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:42.400
and just how to set yourself up as a really
sought-after, premier brand. Yeah, premier

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:47.160
brand. And some of the mistakes that brands
make where they go down market and they become

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:51.919
a commoditized kind of Ross Dress for Less.
It's part of the reason why the fashion industry

00:05:51.919 --> 00:05:56.340
goes and destroys so many clothes. It's not
because they have leftover clothes. They could

00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:00.669
go and sell them. It's what happened to Guess
Jeans, or any of these other brands where

00:06:00.669 --> 00:06:04.820
they said, "Hey, we have this extra inventory.
Let's discount it. Let's do outlet stores.

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:07.560
Let's do these discounting stores," and it
ruins them.

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:16.949
Tim Ferriss: Then they get anchored in the
mid-market. Tthere's so many different ways

00:06:16.949 --> 00:06:23.780
to differentiate. As one example, we're in
L.A. right now, and I had never been to Erewhon.

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:30.470
So for people who don't know, Erewhon is a
very famous, very expensive —

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:34.150
Kevin Rose: It's not expensive.
Tim Ferriss: Let me paint a picture. I went

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:40.440
in and I went to their hot meal cafeteria
line, and I got a couple of boxes. I mean,

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:44.759
it's two meals, maybe two and a half meals
for me. And I was like, oh, I got a kombucha

00:06:44.759 --> 00:06:49.889
too, and maybe a water. And I think that was
about it. There wasn't much more to it. And

00:06:49.889 --> 00:06:54.410
it was $147. I wanted to go there just for
the spectacle, because —

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:55.512
Kevin Rose: I mean, there's a dating scene
there as well.

00:06:55.512 --> 00:06:59.770
Tim Ferriss: There's this dating scene, it's
— yeah. The point I was going to make is

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:03.539
I was having a conversation with someone and
I told them this story and they said, "Wow,

00:07:03.539 --> 00:07:07.980
that's insane. I imagine if you took the same
inventory, you could sell it for 20 percent

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:12.830
less and do really well." And I said, "You
could, but if Erewhon lowered their prices,

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:19.960
they would be dead, I think." Because part
of the allure, part of the story, part of

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:29.069
the word of mouth is how expensive it is.
I mean, look, they have other stuff that is,

00:07:29.069 --> 00:07:34.479
I'm sure, of incredibly high quality. And
I respect that also. But it has become, the

00:07:34.479 --> 00:07:41.650
reason I hear about Erewhon in a place like
Austin or other places in the country is highly

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:47.870
exclusive, very expensive. And I'm not saying
that as a knock.

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:52.490
Kevin Rose: Well, just talking about grocery
stores, even though this isn't that most that

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:55.260
interesting of a topic —
Tim Ferriss: But this maps to a bunch of other

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:57.680
things.
Kevin Rose: Oh, for sure. Yeah. But I think

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:02.350
when you see Whole Foods get bought by Amazon
and it becomes this kind of mass scaled enterprise,

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:06.970
although it was, you could argue beforehand,
when you go in there, it's not the level of

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:13.419
quality it was a decade ago. It's just not,
it's been degraded. And this is now that next

00:08:13.419 --> 00:08:14.449
new level.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:08:14.449 --> 00:08:18.960
Kevin Rose: And I would say that when you
go into Erewhon, it doesn't feel bougie though.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.180
It doesn't feel like crazy, just luxury for
luxury's sake.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:27.260
Tim Ferriss: What you mean by bougie? Because
that word gets, I've heard bougie said more

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:29.740
times than I can count since I got to L.A.
about a week ago.

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:33.390
Kevin Rose: I think it's just kind of like
the one thing, I've only been in L.A. a few

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:38.209
months, and the one thing that I love about
it actually is that it's a choose your own

00:08:38.209 --> 00:08:43.120
adventure. So you can go as crazy high-end
as you want to, whatever you want to, however

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:45.980
you define that.
Or you can find an awesome little dive bar

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:52.130
with a great cocktail. And so I actually like
that. And for me, I tend to float somewhere

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:56.690
in the middle. Don't get me wrong, I like
a nice, awesome steak in a great killer steakhouse,

00:08:56.690 --> 00:09:01.130
which they have a lot of those out here. But
at the same time, it's like it's different

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:04.910
than Portland. Like Portland, we didn't have
the high end. We had a lot of the middle.

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:10.100
So it's like, Erewhon is very small. It's
a very small grocery store.

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:12.080
Tim Ferriss: It's super small. I was actually
expecting it to be much larger.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:14.690
Kevin Rose: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: So I'd go there, and I've been

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:19.820
there since. And it is high quality. The food
is high quality. I think the name is also,

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:23.610
if I'm getting this right, an anagram of "nowhere"
for people who may be wondering about the

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:24.610
name.
Kevin Rose: Interesting.

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:30.480
Tim Ferriss: The experience that I had actually
yesterday brought a lot of this to top of

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:36.459
mind for me. And my experience yesterday was
going to this place called The Magic Castle

00:09:36.459 --> 00:09:37.870
for the second time.
Kevin Rose: Nice. You went.

00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:41.690
Tim Ferriss: I did. And for those who don't
know about The Magic Castle, I'm not going

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:51.260
to do it justice, but it is the mecca of magic
in the sense that people from all over the

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:57.060
world, the kids who become obsessed with magic,
sleight of hand, illusion, et cetera, the

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:04.260
one place, if they've heard of it, that everyone
wants to go is The Magic Castle. And anyone

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:09.590
who's anyone, more or less, in that world
blows through the doors probably once a year.

00:10:09.590 --> 00:10:11.900
Kevin Rose: Did you dress up?
Tim Ferriss: You have to dress up.

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:13.120
Kevin Rose: You have to wear a tie, right?
Tim Ferriss: This is where I was going. You

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:15.350
have to wear a suit and tie, not just tie,
suit and tie.

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:19.990
I did not come here with a suit and tie, nor
did I come here with dress shoes. So a friend

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:23.950
of mine who was visiting, who also didn't
have any of the fancy clothes because the

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:26.710
opportunity to go came up very last minute.
Kevin Rose: Yeah.

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:31.110
Tim Ferriss: Men's Warehouse, we went to Hollywood
Suits on Hollywood Boulevard.

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:33.600
Kevin Rose: You rented?
Tim Ferriss: We, no, we couldn't rent. So

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:39.440
we bought suits. But here's the thing. From
the moment we walked in, and I know these

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:43.190
are quality problems, the fact that you can
buy a suit that you never intend to wear again

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:47.800
is kind of insane. But I knew that it would
cost close to the same to rent and that we

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:50.920
didn't have time to rent because literally
we found out that we had the opportunity to

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:56.450
go to this amazing show. It's a very small
show with an incredible magician who I'll

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:58.620
mention here because the show is absolutely
spectacular.

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:02.980
And then I'll rewind, so I'm going to get
his name probably pronounced wrong, but Simon

00:11:02.980 --> 00:11:08.940
Coronel, C-O-R-O-N-E-L. People, if you don't
know this name, you are going to know this

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:11.750
name. This guy put on one of the best shows
I've ever seen.

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:14.052
Kevin Rose: Young person or?
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, he's a young, I mean, I

00:11:14.052 --> 00:11:18.389
don't know exactly how old he is. Maybe he's
my age. So I'll call that young. Yeah, just

00:11:18.389 --> 00:11:23.620
the older I get, the younger, I think my current
age is. But we went to Hollywood Suits. We

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:29.050
had literally, I want to say 90 minutes. And
we walked in, we didn't even have 90 minutes.

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:35.320
We had an hour. We walked in, we just said,
"Here are our sizes. Where are your suits?"

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:38.139
Went over, grabbed whatever. Looked reasonably
good off the rack.

00:11:38.139 --> 00:11:39.980
Kevin Rose: I hate that. Were they itchy?
I hate that —

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:43.370
Tim Ferriss: Well, hold on. So we get, then
it's like, what size is your neck? Shirt?

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:48.820
Boom. The guy throws it on top. Because it's
sort of a rack and stack high volume spot.

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:52.820
Right. And then it's like shoes, belt, this,
that, and the other thing. And we walked out

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:57.050
the door with everything for between $150
and $200 a suit. Okay.

00:11:57.050 --> 00:12:01.790
Kevin Rose: That's insane. That's awesome.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. So then they can't do the,

00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:06.269
we can't do the hemming there. So you have
to go down this alley, around the corner and

00:12:06.269 --> 00:12:09.230
you think you might be murdered, but no, you
find somebody who's in this room.

00:12:09.230 --> 00:12:10.510
Kevin Rose: You could staple it though. I've
done that before.

00:12:10.510 --> 00:12:14.019
Tim Ferriss: You could staple it. We didn't
have staplers though. We have to call an Uber

00:12:14.019 --> 00:12:21.060
in 30 minutes to get to this place and we
get everything done. Literally, we walk in

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:27.310
the door and then 45 minutes later we're in
our suits and we go to The Magic Castle.

00:12:27.310 --> 00:12:33.610
And my friend and I both have, I don't have
a lot of suits, right? But I have one or two

00:12:33.610 --> 00:12:39.029
nice suits for weddings and things like that.
They're not cheap and they're fitted and this,

00:12:39.029 --> 00:12:43.931
that and the other thing. And both of us looked
at the suits and they're, okay, these are

00:12:43.931 --> 00:12:49.699
not the best suits in the world, but they're
surprisingly fitting and surprisingly good.

00:12:49.699 --> 00:12:55.630
And I got a compliment on my suit while I
was there. And it raises questions, right?

00:12:55.630 --> 00:13:04.240
When it's like, okay, this was $150-200. So
what differentiates? And yes, they're better

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:09.250
materials. Yes, there's better fitting, but
then you're waiting like five months for alterations

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:14.120
or whatever. When you get into the super fancy
class and you're like, okay, that's not about

00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:18.740
entirely quality. That's about the story of
the weight. That's about being able to tell

00:13:18.740 --> 00:13:23.480
your friends the process you went through
to get the thing, which is not to diminish

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:26.710
its value.
It's just to say it's really interesting that

00:13:26.710 --> 00:13:32.650
so much, such a high percentage of the total
cost could be placed on that. So I think about

00:13:32.650 --> 00:13:36.150
these things.
Do you think you're going to get into any

00:13:36.150 --> 00:13:41.920
other high-end stuff? Although I want to back
up to your comment that to get the equivalent

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:44.529
in terms of grade for wine —
Kevin Rose: Yeah.

00:13:44.529 --> 00:13:47.790
Tim Ferriss: — would just be impossibly
cost-prohibitive, right? You're just going

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:52.440
to have to pay out the nose and take out a
second mortgage. So affordable luxuries, in

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:59.180
terms of affordable luxuries, anything else
that you enjoy that you would sort of put

00:13:59.180 --> 00:14:05.990
in this same class? For me, one would be chocolate.
You can get, what some would consider the

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:10.850
best chocolate in the world, and if you're
willing to spend even 20 bucks, even 50.

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:12.600
Kevin Rose: What's your go-to? I like Dick
Taylor.

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:15.610
Tim Ferriss: I used to be, all right.
I want to hear yours. I haven't been in this

00:14:15.610 --> 00:14:20.640
world for a long time, but about 10 years
ago I really got into it. This is when I was

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:25.110
actually, I guess a little bit earlier, maybe
11-12 years when I was working on The 4-Hour

00:14:25.110 --> 00:14:30.160
Chef. Yeah. I got into this and looked at
it very seriously. Coffee, you can really

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:33.360
get incredible coffee if you're willing to
pay up just a little bit.

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:41.120
Kevin Rose: Yeah, absolutely. Yeah. The coffee
is one that I do pay up for I, or a subscription,

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:46.269
which is their single origin, high-end coffee
from a roaster called Proud Mary. And they're

00:14:46.269 --> 00:14:51.459
based in Australia, but they have a location
in Portland, Oregon as well. And so they have

00:14:51.459 --> 00:14:54.389
a varietal of coffee called a Geisha coffee.

00:14:54.389 --> 00:14:56.470
