01:11:46.989 --> 01:11:48.901
Tim Ferriss: Oh, wow. That's cool.
Kevin Rose: — that you can get. So —

01:11:48.901 --> 01:11:51.590
Tim Ferriss: Oh, that's awesome.
Kevin Rose: Yeah, exactly. So it's —

01:11:51.590 --> 01:11:54.440
Tim Ferriss: This brings back so many memories.
Kevin Rose: That's official from Nintendo.

01:11:54.440 --> 01:11:59.020
Because you could always find the clone USB
Nintendo controllers that were the fakes and

01:11:59.020 --> 01:12:02.159
all that. That has the same feel. You push
the buttons.

01:12:02.159 --> 01:12:04.330
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Okay.
Kevin Rose: Everything about it is legit.

01:12:04.330 --> 01:12:06.270
Tim Ferriss: Oh, man. That's —
Kevin Rose: And they charge when you slide

01:12:06.270 --> 01:12:08.440
them on the side of the Switch.
Tim Ferriss: They charge. That's brilliant.

01:12:08.440 --> 01:12:10.080
Kevin Rose: When you link USB. It's so cool.
Tim Ferriss: That's brilliant.

01:12:10.080 --> 01:12:12.410
Kevin Rose: Right. Isn't that awesome?
Tim Ferriss: That's super awesome.

01:12:12.410 --> 01:12:15.969
Kevin Rose: So they have their pass now, where
you can get all the original Nintendo games.

01:12:15.969 --> 01:12:16.969
And —
Tim Ferriss: That's fun.

01:12:16.969 --> 01:12:22.180
Kevin Rose: — it's crazy. I was on their
marketplace. And my dad's passed away, and

01:12:22.180 --> 01:12:26.409
one of the things that I remember as a child
is there was the original Nintendo Pinball

01:12:26.409 --> 01:12:30.949
game. And my dad used to watch me play. He
had, actually had a little tiny TV for me

01:12:30.949 --> 01:12:36.680
so I could play next to him as he's watching
his shows or whatever. And I remember he never

01:12:36.680 --> 01:12:41.460
wanted to play any games with me. He was an
older dad, and he didn't really get into Nintendo,

01:12:41.460 --> 01:12:45.580
but when Pinball came up, he was like, "Okay,
let me grab the controller." And so he played

01:12:45.580 --> 01:12:48.510
Nintendo with me. So when I saw that, I was
like, "Oh, my God." I mean, it's so much nostalgia.

01:12:48.510 --> 01:12:50.510
Tim Ferriss: What nostalgia. Yeah, exactly.
Kevin Rose: So much nostalgia.

01:12:50.510 --> 01:12:54.430
Tim Ferriss: I'll just teach a word for folks
who may be interested in Japanese. They use

01:12:54.430 --> 01:13:02.860
it a lot more than we say nostalgia. But they
would say "natsukashii." And that's sort of

01:13:02.860 --> 01:13:10.429
got the same feel as "nostalgic," but they
use it kind of like "saudade" in Brazilian

01:13:10.429 --> 01:13:16.070
Portuguese. But it's roughly like, "Ah. How
nice." Like, "Aw, that's really nostalgic.

01:13:16.070 --> 01:13:19.420
That brings back the memories."
Kevin Rose: There was a blog post that I saw

01:13:19.420 --> 01:13:24.830
where it was 25 words that exist in Japanese
that don't have English meanings.

01:13:24.830 --> 01:13:26.640
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Kevin Rose: And they're so beautiful.

01:13:26.640 --> 01:13:30.130
Tim Ferriss: They have some nice ones.
Kevin Rose: They capture these amazing just

01:13:30.130 --> 01:13:35.659
moments in time. There was this one that was
like "The sunlight as it falls between…"

01:13:35.659 --> 01:13:37.449
Tim Ferriss: Branches.
Kevin Rose: Branches, yes.

01:13:37.449 --> 01:13:39.239
Tim Ferriss: "Komorebi."
Kevin Rose: Yes. Yes.

01:13:39.239 --> 01:13:43.300
Tim Ferriss: "Komorebi" is like "Dappled sunlight
coming through tree branches and leaves."

01:13:43.300 --> 01:13:45.670
Kevin Rose: It's so good. It's so good.
Tim Ferriss: It's so amazing.

01:13:45.670 --> 01:13:47.870
Kevin Rose: They have a word for that.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Komorebi.

01:13:47.870 --> 01:13:50.090
Kevin Rose: That's so amazing.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it's super beautiful.

01:13:50.090 --> 01:13:52.960
Kevin Rose: Oh. So, I was in Tokyo.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Let's talk about it.

01:13:52.960 --> 01:13:55.449
Kevin Rose: Celebrated my 10-year wedding
anniversary out there.

01:13:55.449 --> 01:13:59.659
Tim Ferriss: Congratulations again. Yes.
Kevin Rose: That was amazing. And we had a

01:13:59.659 --> 01:14:04.790
ton of fun. Darya met me out there. We did
a Moonbirds meetup. So we had over a hundred

01:14:04.790 --> 01:14:08.810
Japanese-speaking Moonbirds collectors out
there, which was challenging and awesome at

01:14:08.810 --> 01:14:14.460
the same time. And then Henry, my Zen instructor
here —

01:14:14.460 --> 01:14:18.020
Tim Ferriss: Shukman.
Kevin Rose: Shukman, here in the United States,

01:14:18.020 --> 01:14:25.739
introduced me to the head of his lineage of
Zen, who I got to go out and meet with and

01:14:25.739 --> 01:14:28.670
sit down with and have a little private —
Tim Ferriss: Go to karaoke?

01:14:28.670 --> 01:14:33.389
Kevin Rose: Yeah, exactly. Went out drinking
and just did karaoke together all night long.

01:14:33.389 --> 01:14:38.830
No, but we had a little private gathering
and sit-down and did a private —

01:14:38.830 --> 01:14:41.969
Tim Ferriss: Did he lay his hands on you?
Exorcise the demons?

01:14:41.969 --> 01:14:51.260
Kevin Rose: He helped me with my practice.
His English is quite good. It was very intimidating.

01:14:51.260 --> 01:14:52.659
Very intimidating.
Tim Ferriss: I bet. That's actually —

01:14:52.659 --> 01:14:55.850
Kevin Rose: This is the head of the Sanbo
Zen, which is —

01:14:55.850 --> 01:14:57.260
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, Sanbo.
Kevin Rose: — a big —

01:14:57.260 --> 01:15:00.600
Tim Ferriss: Sanbo. That's right. Yeah.
Kevin Rose: — three treasures. A big lineage

01:15:00.600 --> 01:15:08.260
of Zen. And I'm sitting here with this guy,
and he's just asking me very pointed questions

01:15:08.260 --> 01:15:09.264
about my —
Tim Ferriss: Like what?

01:15:09.264 --> 01:15:11.630
Kevin Rose: — intention in Zen.
Tim Ferriss: "What is your intention with

01:15:11.630 --> 01:15:14.340
my daughter?" What's —
Kevin Rose: Yeah, more or less. But it was

01:15:14.340 --> 01:15:16.840
very cutting questions, dude, like —
Tim Ferriss: Like what?

01:15:16.840 --> 01:15:21.580
Kevin Rose: Like a surgeon just coming in
and just — I don't know if I should say

01:15:21.580 --> 01:15:39.210
the things because it's this private interview.
I'll give you an example. It's more or less

01:15:39.210 --> 01:15:46.969
like, "Why are you here? Where are you?" You
know how in Zen, they have these — we've

01:15:46.969 --> 01:15:49.800
talked about these koans before. You've done
interviews with Henry, and thank you for doing

01:15:49.800 --> 01:15:54.670
those — where it's like these moments where
a really good Zen practitioner — or not

01:15:54.670 --> 01:15:58.290
practitioner, but a good Zen master can come
in and look at a student and know kind of

01:15:58.290 --> 01:16:01.199
exactly the little nudge they need to give
them.

01:16:01.199 --> 01:16:03.050
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Kevin Rose: There's these moments where they

01:16:03.050 --> 01:16:07.010
call it, there's these stories in Zen where
it'll be like they grab your coat and yank

01:16:07.010 --> 01:16:13.100
you in a certain direction just to get you
to snap, wake up, this moment, just boom.

01:16:13.100 --> 01:16:14.810
They want you to pop.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:16:14.810 --> 01:16:20.440
Kevin Rose: And it was very much that like,
why are you here? What do you want from this?

01:16:20.440 --> 01:16:23.710
Boom, boom, boom.
Tim Ferriss: I think the guy on the street

01:16:23.710 --> 01:16:28.480
when I walked back to my Airbnb in Venice
asked me those questions.

01:16:28.480 --> 01:16:34.901
Kevin Rose: Exactly, but less fentanyl-driven.
But yeah, so it was amazing. It was very —

01:16:34.901 --> 01:16:38.270
Tim Ferriss: HI hear the Zen masters have
cut back on their fentanyl a lot.

01:16:38.270 --> 01:16:40.390
Kevin Rose: Yeah. This guy was operating on
a different plane.

01:16:40.390 --> 01:16:44.310
Tim Ferriss: So what did that feel like for
you? Why did you want to do it? First of all,

01:16:44.310 --> 01:16:47.870
because.
Kevin Rose: Just Henry had said such great

01:16:47.870 --> 01:16:55.630
things about him that, and Henry is, he will
never say this publicly, but I believe he

01:16:55.630 --> 01:16:57.050
is one —
Tim Ferriss: But I'll say it for him.

01:16:57.050 --> 01:17:02.699
Kevin Rose: No, I will. Because I think it's
important. He's such a modest guy that Henry

01:17:02.699 --> 01:17:09.100
is one of, I think it's five or so fully sanctions
and masters in this lineage. And that's a

01:17:09.100 --> 01:17:16.620
big deal when there's hundreds of teachers
in this realm. So this is his master, his

01:17:16.620 --> 01:17:20.900
teacher, and if you get a chance to take that
meeting, you take it. So I went in there like,

01:17:20.900 --> 01:17:27.870
"Nah, I'm busy. I'm going to try on some new
shoes." I'm too busy going to try some fancy

01:17:27.870 --> 01:17:33.650
Japanese coffee, but which I did, which is
amazing. Cafe Mameya out there is fantastic.

01:17:33.650 --> 01:17:38.969
But honestly though, the one thing that I
took away from it for people that I will share

01:17:38.969 --> 01:17:42.598
is that one of the things that he asked me
is, he's like, "How often do you practice

01:17:42.598 --> 01:17:46.550
and how long do you practice?"
And I said, "I practice 25 minutes a day,

01:17:46.550 --> 01:17:50.000
probably five days a week." And he goes, "What
about the other two days?" And I go, "Well,

01:17:50.000 --> 01:17:54.239
I've got a startup, I've got this and I've
got that." And he goes, "I don't care." And

01:17:54.239 --> 01:17:57.540
I'm paraphrasing here, but he goes, "I don't
care if you've practiced five minutes a day."

01:17:57.540 --> 01:18:04.800
He goes, "Don't miss a day, don't break a
day, continue to do it. Don't miss a day."

01:18:04.800 --> 01:18:10.889
And since then, I have not missed a day. And
I will say it's kind of amazing because even

01:18:10.889 --> 01:18:16.239
if you can sit down for five minutes, it just
keeps that continuity going. And it actually

01:18:16.239 --> 01:18:21.350
keeps me at a slightly elevated more, I don't
know. It keeps the commitment stronger, it

01:18:21.350 --> 01:18:25.680
feels better. And that was sound advice.
Tim Ferriss: That's great advice. Darya, can

01:18:25.680 --> 01:18:30.870
I trouble for just a little bit of water,
please? Thank you so much.

01:18:30.870 --> 01:18:34.960
Kevin Rose: Arigatou gozaimasu.
Tim Ferriss: Hai. Arigatou gozaimasu.

01:18:34.960 --> 01:18:39.060
Kevin Rose: What? Wait, what's water?
Tim Ferriss: You can do it.

01:18:39.060 --> 01:18:48.020
Kevin Rose: Hold on, hold on. Don't tell me.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, you got it.

01:18:48.020 --> 01:18:51.719
Kevin Rose: Oishii! It's so oishii!
Tim Ferriss: What?

01:18:51.719 --> 01:18:54.240
Kevin Rose: Oishii.
Tim Ferriss: No, that's tasty.

01:18:54.240 --> 01:18:57.360
Kevin Rose: Delicious. Yeah, I know. The water's
tasty.

01:18:57.360 --> 01:19:01.070
Kevin Rose: What? Mizu!
Tim Ferriss: Mizu. There are a lot of mizus

01:19:01.070 --> 01:19:03.630
out there.
Tim Ferriss: So let's stay on the Japanese

01:19:03.630 --> 01:19:04.630
kick for a second.
Kevin Rose: Yeah.

01:19:04.630 --> 01:19:09.120
Tim Ferriss: This is a book in front of me,
which I'm just digging into.

01:19:09.120 --> 01:19:13.020
Kevin Rose: Japanese Death Poems. Let's talk
about Japanese Death Poems.

01:19:13.020 --> 01:19:18.250
Tim Ferriss: I did not actually, I'm embarrassed
to admit, know this was a thing. This was

01:19:18.250 --> 01:19:26.389
given to me by a friend who has spent a lot
of time in the military and has developed

01:19:26.389 --> 01:19:35.120
a rather unique perspective on death from,
I suppose, the vantage point of a lay person,

01:19:35.120 --> 01:19:43.159
right? Of a civilian. And so this book is,
I'll just read the quote on the back. This

01:19:43.159 --> 01:19:47.550
is one of the blurbs.
"A wonderful introduction to the Japanese

01:19:47.550 --> 01:19:52.179
tradition of Jisei. This volume is crowned
with exquisite spontaneous verse and pith

01:19:52.179 --> 01:19:56.830
often hilarious descriptions of the eccentric
and committed monastics who wrote the poems."

01:19:56.830 --> 01:20:03.830
So these poems are written on the verge of
death. And literally the subtitle is Written

01:20:03.830 --> 01:20:11.590
by Zen Monks and Haiku Poets on the Verge
of Death. And it's incredibly well researched.

01:20:11.590 --> 01:20:16.370
You have not just the English versions, you
have the introduction and all the context.

01:20:16.370 --> 01:20:21.850
You have the Japanese and then you have footnotes
describing what these various words might

01:20:21.850 --> 01:20:32.120
mean, what the metaphors allude to. And it's
really just a phenomenal window into an aspect

01:20:32.120 --> 01:20:37.350
of Japanese culture that I had no exposure
to. I would've expected that I would've been

01:20:37.350 --> 01:20:43.159
exposed to this before, but I'm looking forward
to diving into this. And I've already read

01:20:43.159 --> 01:20:48.010
perhaps the first 10 pages.
So this is Japanese Death Poems, by Tuttle

01:20:48.010 --> 01:20:52.280
Publishing compiled and with an introduction
by Yoel Hoffmann.

01:20:52.280 --> 01:20:56.179
Kevin Rose: So I have a handful, I don't know
if I've told you this, but I have a handful

01:20:56.179 --> 01:21:00.230
of haiku books that I've read by a Japanese
masters.

01:21:00.230 --> 01:21:03.210
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Kevin Rose: And one of the things I always

01:21:03.210 --> 01:21:10.219
felt was just so beautiful about them is they
always include their kind of death poems at

01:21:10.219 --> 01:21:14.889
the very end. So you always get, it's oftentimes
on their deathbed when they're about to pass

01:21:14.889 --> 01:21:20.239
what is the last thing that they wrote down.
And it's often in haiku form if they're a

01:21:20.239 --> 01:21:21.929
haiku master, right?
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, totally.

01:21:21.929 --> 01:21:24.600
Kevin Rose: And so it's just, it's beautiful
stuff.

01:21:24.600 --> 01:21:28.850
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, there's some really Japanese
stuff in here, right? This is died 19 — I'm

01:21:28.850 --> 01:21:33.030
sorry, "Died in 1698": "For not honoring my
parents / while I lived, in my last hour / I

01:21:33.030 --> 01:21:41.409
feel remorse." That is very super Japanese.
"The autumn hues / of knotweed seem / like

01:21:41.409 --> 01:21:46.330
cups of wine." Yeah.
Kevin Rose: Are you a big fan of haiku?

01:21:46.330 --> 01:21:53.889
Tim Ferriss: I am. This is my first time really
being exposed to a lot of Japanese, and it

01:21:53.889 --> 01:21:58.760
does sound better in Japanese. There's a certain
cadence to it.

01:21:58.760 --> 01:22:03.500
So I'm not going to get the pronunciation
quite perfectly here, but it like if you have,

01:22:03.500 --> 01:22:10.909
okay, so Hakuro is this person, died on the
19th day of the 12th month, 1766. So the English,

01:22:10.909 --> 01:22:13.989
and I'm going to fuck this up in Japanese,
so I apologize to any Japanese speakers out

01:22:13.989 --> 01:22:19.120
there, but the English, just listen to the
sounds, like the cadence. So the English is

01:22:19.120 --> 01:22:26.659
"An ailing mallard / falls through the chilly
night / and teeters off." But then the Japanese

01:22:26.659 --> 01:22:35.620
is "Yamu kari no / yosamu ni orite / obotsukana,"
right? So it just has a cleaner, crisper cadence

01:22:35.620 --> 01:22:39.449
to it, which doesn't mean you shouldn't do
the English, but this is my first time being

01:22:39.449 --> 01:22:45.100
exposed to a lot of haiku in Japanese, which
is fun for me. Number one makes very hard

01:22:45.100 --> 01:22:53.210
for me to decipher in many cases, but they
have a beautiful sound to them, so.

01:22:53.210 --> 01:22:55.880
Kevin Rose: That's awesome. I have to pick
that book up.

01:22:55.880 --> 01:23:00.620
Tim Ferriss: Death Poems.
Kevin Rose: There's a great poem about haiku

01:23:00.620 --> 01:23:02.860
and about the masters called Three Simple
Lines.

01:23:02.860 --> 01:23:04.490
Tim Ferriss: Okay.
Kevin Rose: Have you ever read that?

01:23:04.490 --> 01:23:07.120
Tim Ferriss: No.
Kevin Rose: It's a fantastic book that is

01:23:07.120 --> 01:23:13.219
written by a Zen practitioner that covers
a lot of the Zen masters of haiku and along

01:23:13.219 --> 01:23:19.240
with a really beautiful personal story. Very
short read. Definitely worth picking up along

01:23:19.240 --> 01:23:21.429
with this.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I mean, how Japanese relate

01:23:21.429 --> 01:23:28.100
to death, which is something I have some exposure
to, but not to the poems. This particular

01:23:28.100 --> 01:23:36.600
Jisei format tells you a lot about how they
live. I mean, it does provide sort of a prism

01:23:36.600 --> 01:23:44.650
through which you can appreciate how they
navigate a lot of life as well in addressing

01:23:44.650 --> 01:23:51.080
the final hours and thinking about dying,
death, the path to death.

01:23:51.080 --> 01:23:54.739
When you get a better understanding of that,
many of the things that you observe in Japanese

01:23:54.739 --> 01:24:01.290
culture make more sense, in a way.
Have you ever done an escape room?

01:24:01.290 --> 01:24:03.210
Kevin Rose: Yes.
Tim Ferriss: You have?

01:24:03.210 --> 01:24:04.400
Kevin Rose: Yes.
Tim Ferriss: How many?

01:24:04.400 --> 01:24:05.420
Kevin Rose: Multiple.
Tim Ferriss: Really?

01:24:05.420 --> 01:24:07.120
Kevin Rose: Yes.
Tim Ferriss: Multiple. What does that mean?

01:24:07.120 --> 01:24:08.810
Like a hundred?
Kevin Rose: Like four.

01:24:08.810 --> 01:24:12.550
Tim Ferriss: Four. All right. So by the —
Kevin Rose: They're so stupid.

01:24:12.550 --> 01:24:16.190
Tim Ferriss: The fatigued way. So I, all right.
"They're so stupid," he says.

01:24:16.190 --> 01:24:19.850
Kevin Rose: They're horrible.
Tim Ferriss: I did my first escape room today.

01:24:19.850 --> 01:24:22.460
I had a fucking blast. I don't know what's
wrong with you. You're getting old and boring.

01:24:22.460 --> 01:24:26.449
Kevin Rose: No, it's just like you're going
in there and it's like, what are you doing?

01:24:26.449 --> 01:24:31.659
You can get out if you want to. Are they really
locking you up? No.

01:24:31.659 --> 01:24:35.790
Tim Ferriss: It's called a game, Kevin.
Kevin Rose: Yeah, I know. But —

01:24:35.790 --> 01:24:38.949
Tim Ferriss: When you're playing Zelda, you're
trying to do shit in a video game. You don't

01:24:38.949 --> 01:24:43.060
actually need to do those things.
Kevin Rose: No, but I just, Darya can explain

01:24:43.060 --> 01:24:46.800
it. You know what I'm talking about. You go
in there and they lock you up. It's a little

01:24:46.800 --> 01:24:51.489
room and they're like, we really quick grab
the book and open up the book and turn to

01:24:51.489 --> 01:24:56.070
chapter 12, and people are yelling shit out.
And you're just like, I don't want to do this.

01:24:56.070 --> 01:25:00.080
I just want to have a drink.
Tim Ferriss: All right. So I'm going to provide

01:25:00.080 --> 01:25:06.000
a contrast in styles here. So Kevin, who got
so outraged that I implied that he drank alcohol

01:25:06.000 --> 01:25:08.710
earlier —
Kevin Rose: No, I just, all my friends want

01:25:08.710 --> 01:25:11.909
me to do it sober. And I'm like, why are we
doing this sober? And they're like, you can

01:25:11.909 --> 01:25:14.980
get through it faster. We could break a world
record. I'm like, this is dumb.

01:25:14.980 --> 01:25:19.889
Tim Ferriss: Look, doing it in a hyper-competitive
way I think is probably not my jam. But I

01:25:19.889 --> 01:25:24.889
had the chance to do escape rooms for the
first time today. I went to this place called

01:25:24.889 --> 01:25:30.290
Escape Revolution, which is here in L.A.,
Elan Lee, who is one of the co-founders of

01:25:30.290 --> 01:25:34.620
Exploding Kittens, was kind enough to invite
me. I don't want to name other people who

01:25:34.620 --> 01:25:40.719
were there because maybe they don't want to
be named. But it was a small group and I thought

01:25:40.719 --> 01:25:46.850
it was absolutely fantastic. You have an hour,
there's some type of pretext or story, there's

01:25:46.850 --> 01:25:51.719
a narrative arc. So in our first instance,
and I'm not going to give away any of the

01:25:51.719 --> 01:25:58.730
tools, so I won't provide any teasers, but
we had a real armored car that's locked in

01:25:58.730 --> 01:26:00.449
a garage.
Kevin Rose: That's cooler than mine.

01:26:00.449 --> 01:26:06.280
Tim Ferriss: And you have to, you're robbers
and you need to get the money out of the car.

01:26:06.280 --> 01:26:10.100
But the mechanic who set up all the security
systems ran off because the police were on

01:26:10.100 --> 01:26:14.199
the way. So you have 60 minutes to figure
it out.

01:26:14.199 --> 01:26:21.429
And we had a blast. And this place is built
in such a way that the set design and the

01:26:21.429 --> 01:26:28.469
sound effects and the entire experience makes
it, I don't want to say cinematic, but it's

01:26:28.469 --> 01:26:34.470
just a very compelling way to become immersed
in a story. And you have to collaborate, I

01:26:34.470 --> 01:26:40.790
also, part of what made it fun is that I have
a game designer in Elan Lee. I had a number

01:26:40.790 --> 01:26:46.720
of other people who were very accustomed to
mechanics of different types. Not car mechanics,

01:26:46.720 --> 01:26:51.890
but game mechanics and so on. A woman who
had done almost 2,000 escape rooms herself.

